#
# CHANGES -- fetchconfig-web
#
# fetchconfig-web is a web front-end for the fetchconfig network device
# configuration backup tool. Newest changes first.
#

1.14
====
+ Tools: new "Template viewer" -- lists the generic-model *.tmpl templates in
  the directories fetchconfig.pl -t reports (de-duplicated by full path, sorted
  by name) and opens each read-only with template syntax highlighting with a role-based colour scheme: state + reserved state_* entry states in
  blue, user-defined state names bold blue, directives teal, expect green, the
  send* family red, goto/done purple, capture_start/stop orange, nop/arrow grey,
  barewords lavender. The list shows the full template name (path incl. file). The path
  is whitelisted against the discovered templates (no "..", must match a scanned
  path), so it cannot read outside the template directories.
+ Added a Makefile.PL and a t/ unit-test suite (Test::More). "perl
  Makefile.PL" checks the required Perl modules up front and fails with clear
  errors if any is missing or too old (DBI >= 1.641, DBD::Pg >= 3.15.0,
  CGI >= 4.53, Algorithm::Diff). README documents an AIX-specific CGI 4.53
  CGI::Util patch for a harmless start_html() warning (may also apply on Linux).
+ Added a "make doc" target that regenerates the HTML manual via render-doc.py
  (errors clearly if no Python interpreter is installed). run "perl
  Makefile.PL && make test" or "prove -I t/lib t/". Covers version compare/gate,
  backup filename parsing, secret masking + HTML/JSON escaping, valid_id, the
  model-option catalog, template listing + generic-model validation, and the
  syntax-highlighter safety invariant. "make install" only prints a note (the
  app is deployed by copying files, per README.md).
+ Added imageconvert.pl (with README.imageconvert): a standalone helper that
  encodes an image as a base64 "my $VAR = do {...};" Perl snippet (80-column
  block) for embedding in the CGI, with help/version screens and error checks.
* Replaced the browser's native confirmation dialogs with a custom modal styled
  like the Backup-Now spinner box (centred card on a dark overlay). Destructive
  actions (deletes, "permanently") get a red Confirm button; benign ones a blue
  one, and a red warning-triangle icon (danger) or green check icon (normal)
  beside the message. Applies to all confirmations: orphan/empty-dir/backup/
  old-backup/user deletes, restore, bulk replace, delete-entry and Backup Now.
* The unified-diff "Compare selected" page now uses the full screen width, like
  the single-config view. The backup list, the fetchconfig-log viewer and the
  device-table backup view were widened the same way; the Help page now uses
  80% of the screen width.
+ New template-driven "generic" model. In the device table it is selected with
  model=generic plus a model=<template> option naming the template; the editor
  renders model= as a drop-down of the templates reported by
  "fetchconfig.pl -t" (run once per Setup page, id number stripped, sorted;
  falls back to a text field if none are found). The dropdown appears on both
  the default: and device forms. model= is required on a generic device line
  only when no "default: generic" line supplies it.
* Model-option audit against the fetchconfig README: timeout is now optional
  on every model (fetchconfig defaults it to 30s); added optional enable +
  show_cmd to cisco-sg300, optional enable to procurve/procurve-ssh, optional
  show_cmd to dell, optional debug to comware-ssh, optional pager_cmd to
  mediant-sbc; removed an erroneous mandatory enable from mikrotik.
* Minimum required fetchconfig version bumped to 9.64.
* fetchconfig-web version bumped to 1.14.

1.12
====
* fetchconfig-web-dbsetup.pl now writes the FONT_BASE_URL and IMAGE_BASE_URL
  keys into the generated config (in the "# --- fetchconfig paths ---" section).
* Login page now shows a full-page backdrop image (IMAGE_BASE_URL/back.jpg)
  behind the title bar, login box and a bottom-centred copyright footer, all of
  which stay legible. Added two config keys, FONT_BASE_URL (default
  /fetchconfig-web/fonts) and IMAGE_BASE_URL (default /fetchconfig/images), for
  the URL paths of the fonts and images directories. Only the login page uses
  the backdrop.
* Minimum required fetchconfig version bumped from 9.57 to 9.60.
* Device-table editor: numeric time fields now show their unit -- "s" for
  timeout/fetch_timeout/banner_timeout, "ms" for prompt_settle_ms/type_delay.
+ New model "mediant-sbc" (AudioCodes Mediant SBC / gateway, Cisco-style CLI):
  user/pass/enable mandatory; optional transport (ssh|telnet|auto), show_cmd,
  debug, banner_timeout, prompt_settle_ms. Adds an "enum_transport" editor
  field type and a dedicated highlighter for AudioCodes' parameter-value
  config syntax.
+ Tools scan tools now run device checks concurrently, controlled by a new
  MAX_PARALLEL_SCAN config option (default 1 = serial; allowed range 1..5; an
  out-of-range value is a configuration error). The scan driver uses a
  client-side worker pool, and the progress line shows how many scan processes
  are currently running (e.g. "120 of 252 -- 4 of 4 scan processes in use").
* Fixed intermittent, device-varying scan failures that appeared once
  MAX_PARALLEL_SCAN > 1 was used. The main cause was the session idle-timeout
  rewrite: it used a truncating open('>') before locking, so a concurrent
  request could read the session file mid-truncate, be treated as logged out,
  and receive the login page instead of JSON. Session files are now rewritten
  atomically (temp file + rename). Also hardened along the way: interrupted
  reads (EINTR) are retried instead of truncating output; the child exit
  status is only trusted when waitpid actually reaped our process; and the
  JSON error-detail is escaped to pure ASCII so a non-ASCII byte in a device's
  error message cannot corrupt the response.
* The scan tools now show why a device could not be checked (the fetchconfig
  exit/stderr detail) next to the device name.
* The single-backup config view now uses the full browser width (5% side
  margins) instead of a fixed 900px, so wide config lines fit without
  scrolling; the config box also keeps clear of the floating Top/Bottom nav.
+ New model "nexus-ssh" (Cisco Nexus / NX-OS over SSH; same options as
  aruba-cx-ssh), with a dedicated NX-OS syntax highlighter (IOS-family token
  rules plus operator-chosen banner delimiters and inline password/key blob
  handling).
* Minimum required fetchconfig version bumped from 9.56 to 9.57.
+ Tools: new "Empty Directory Cleanup" (like Orphaned Configuration Cleanup):
  check (fetchconfig.pl -e) and delete (fetchconfig.pl -e -D) empty repository
  directories. `-e` exits 0 = none, 1 = found; `-e -D` exits 0 on success.
* Minimum required fetchconfig version bumped from 9.55 to 9.56.
* All fetchconfig-transcript pages (Backup Now, empty/suffix backup checks,
  orphaned-backup check and delete) are now ~30% wider so the output box needs
  less horizontal scrolling, and their status lines are consistently coloured
  (green on success, red on failure).
* Documentation HTML now uses the available screen width: the layout is
  wider and tables/code blocks fill the content area, so the CLI reference
  table no longer needs a horizontal scrollbar on wide screens (prose keeps a
  readable measure).
* Documentation: added a "How fetchconfig.pl is invoked" reference (every CLI
  invocation, its arguments, parsed output, and exit codes); restructured the
  security section into a "Security model" with grouped subsections; reviewed
  the README against the current code (nav row, embedded images, feature list,
  Setup/Tools gating); and removed the "Testing performed" section from the
  deliverables.
+ Minimum required fetchconfig version bumped from 9.54 to 9.55.
+ The page footer now shows the installed fetchconfig version, e.g.
  "fetchconfig-web v1.12 using fetchconfig v9.54 -- <copyright>" (omitted if
  the version cannot be determined).
+ Tools: new "Check devices for consistent backup suffixes" scan (like the
  empty-backup scan) that lists devices with a backup-suffix problem, using
  fetchconfig.pl -s (exit 0 = consistent; 1 = suffixes inconsistent;
  2 = consistent but does not match the configured filename_append_suffix).
  Adds a matching "Check backup suffixes" button on each device's backup list
  showing the full fetchconfig.pl -s output and which case applies.
+ Empty-backup check now also recognises fetchconfig's newer info: "wrote
  listing of N zero-byte backup(s) ... to check.out" message.
+ Backup-filename parsing now tolerates fetchconfig's filename_append_suffix:
  the Devices backup list still shows the correct date/time, and "Download
  latest configuration" keeps a dotted suffix (e.g. ".txt") in the download
  name, falling back to ".cfg" when there is none.
* Fixed: the coriant-8600 model wrongly required an "enable" password in the
  device-table editor; per fetchconfig it uses user/pass only, so the enable
  field is no longer shown for it.
+ render-doc.py now runs on both Python 3 and Python 2.7.18 (standard library
  only).
+ Requires fetchconfig 9.54+ (MIN_FETCHCONFIG_VERSION constant). The installed
  version is read from <FETCHCONFIG_PATH>/fetchconfig/Constants.pm (cached,
  refreshed when that file changes) and a warning banner is shown on every
  page after login if it is older, or cannot be determined.
+ Config: the fetchconfig executable is now given as two keys,
  FETCHCONFIG_PATH (install directory, also used to find Constants.pm) and
  FETCHCONFIG_BIN (executable name); it is run as PATH/BIN. The old single
  full-path FETCHCONFIG_BIN form is no longer accepted -- update old configs.
+ Tools / Restore device table: new "Delete backups older than days" bulk
  cleanup (days >= 1, default 30; POST + CSRF + confirm). Deletes
  device-table backups older than the given age by their filename timestamp,
  but ALWAYS keeps the single newest backup even if it is older. The control
  is only shown when at least two backups exist.
+ The app version is now a built-in APP_VERSION constant in
  fetchconfig-web.cgi; the config file's APP_VERSION key optionally
  overrides it (previously the footer was empty without the key).
+ Licensed under the GNU GPL v3 (or later): GPL headers added to both Perl
  programs, a LICENSE file with the full GPLv3 text shipped, and a License
  section added to the README.
* Security hardening (review of 2026-09-17):
  - Device ids and usernames now reject a leading "-" (regex
    ^[\w.][\w.\-]*$ via a central valid_id()), so an id can no longer be
    mistaken for a fetchconfig.pl option (e.g. "-D") when passed as a
    positional argument.
  - Removed acceptance of unsalted {SHA} password hashes; $apr1$, $1$,
    $5$/$6$ and DES crypt remain supported.
  - Session cookie now sets SameSite=Lax (defence-in-depth with CSRF).
  - Failed logins are delayed by 2 seconds (uniformly, to avoid leaking
    whether the user or the password was wrong).
  - The Backup Now cleartext-credential temp file is now auto-removed on
    process exit (File::Temp UNLINK), even if the request dies.
  - SESSION_TTL is now an idle timeout: the expiry slides forward on every
    request instead of being fixed at login.
  - Numeric/boolean config keys (SESSION_TTL, DEVICE_ID_FIELD,
    USE_SUDO_FOR_BACKUP_NOW) are validated and fall back to their defaults
    on invalid values.
* Removed a duplicate .btn-small CSS rule.
+ Device-table editor, Email notification tab: added the optional "port"
  option (SMTP server TCP port; overrides a port given in smtp=host:port,
  else the TLS-mode default is used).
+ Device-table editor, Email notification tab: added the optional "tls"
  option -- a drop-down for the SMTP transport security (off | starttls | ssl).
  The drop-down now appears both when the option is already present and when
  it is added via "+ add option" (added enum/debug/int options render with
  their proper control instead of a plain text field).
+ Device-table editor: the aruba-cx-ssh model gained the optional
  prompt_settle_ms option (milliseconds to wait for output to go idle before
  matching the prompt; default 150, 0 disables).

1.11
====
+ The web UI config file default path is now /etc/fetchconfig-web.cfg (was
  /etc/fetchconfig.cfg); rename the file on upgrade. The path is defined once
  per script and all messages/generated output derive from it.
+ Backup list: new "Download latest configuration" button (beside "Check for
  empty backups") downloads the newest backup's config -- normal devices as
  "<Device-ID>-YYMMDD-HHMMSS.cfg" (timestamp from the backup file), web-binary
  models as their decoded config file.
* Renamed "Check Empty Backups" to "Check for empty backups", and made all
  button labels sentence case app-wide (Backup now, Compare selected, Side by
  side selected, Clear filter).
+ New "Status" page (nav, between Devices and Setup): a table of every
  device's last-run status read from the per-device .status files -- Device-ID
  (clickable), start/end date+time, duration, state and changed. State is
  coloured (success=green, failed=red, skipped=blue) and changed
  (changed/forced=blue, unknown=red, unchanged=normal); devices with no status file show
  N/A. State and Changed have dropdown filters (incl. N/A), plus the same
  sorting, All/10/25/50/100 paging and First/Prev/Next/Last controls as the
  device list, and a "Status table load time" line below the table. Opening a
  device from Status makes the backup page's breadcrumb return to Status.
+ Tools: new "View fetchconfig log" section with a "Display log" button that
  opens the scheduled (cron) fetchconfig.pl output on its own page
  (?action=view_log; path set via the new FETCHCONFIG_LOG config key).
  The log is split into collapsible sections at each -----[NAME]----- header,
  with the name in bold and coloured green (ok) or red (failed, i.e. body
  contains error:/failed); failed sections start expanded, plus Expand all /
  Collapse all buttons. At most LOG_MAX_DEVICES sections (default 1000) are
  shown, with a warning if the log has more. A "Log table load time" line
  (ms/s) is shown below the output. Config: FETCHCONFIG_LOG moved
  under "# --- fetchconfig paths ---" and the binary path is now under
  "# --- fetchconfig binary paths ---".
+ Devices page: the statistics panel now shows "Device table load time"
  (in milliseconds under 1s, otherwise seconds; same small font as the
  footer) as feedback on how long reading and parsing the device table took.
+ Backup Now spinner: if a per-device status file exists (fetchconfig
  >= 9.52 writes "<repository>/<device-id>.status"), the overlay also shows,
  below the runtime counter, the last run's "Last runtime: Ns", "Last backup
  state" (success=green/failed=red/skipped=black) and "Last backup changed"
  (unchanged=green/changed,forced=blue/unknown=black). The repository is
  resolved from the device's repository= option, else its model's default:
  repository=.
+ Backup Now: while the (blocking) backup runs, a full-screen overlay shows
  a spinner and the elapsed runtime in a large font; after 10 seconds it adds
  a note that backing up a device can take a while.
+ Device-table editor, Email notification tab: added the optional "footer"
  option (the copyright/attribution text shown in the summary e-mail's
  footer).
* Fixed: with more than one email: line (fetchconfig allows the line to be
  repeated), the editor wrongly warned that from/to/smtp were missing when
  they were split across lines (e.g. from/to/smtp on one line and footer on
  another). The mandatory-option check now considers the merged set of all
  email: lines.
* email: option values are no longer double-quoted just for containing
  spaces (the email: line is comma-separated, so spaces don't need quoting).
  In particular footer="..." is written bare, e.g. footer=(c) 2026 Acme.
  Values containing a comma are still quoted.

1.10
====
+ New device model "procurve-snmp" (HP ProCurve ProVision incl. 4000M/8000M,
  backed up over SNMP+TFTP). No user/pass -- the mandatory secret is the SNMP
  read-write "community"; also repository/keep mandatory and timeout,
  snmp_version, oid, remote_file, changes_only, on_fetch_run, on_fetch_cat,
  timezone, filename_append_suffix, debug optional. Its backup is text, so it
  uses the normal content view and Compare / Side by Side diffs (no
  highlighting). The community= value is now masked wherever config is shown.
* Fixed: the Copy button on a config view could put non-ASCII text (e.g.
  umlauts) on the clipboard in the wrong encoding over plain HTTP, where the
  async Clipboard API isn't available. The legacy fallback now sets the
  clipboard data as UTF-8 text/plain explicitly.
* Fixed: non-ASCII characters (e.g. German umlauts) in a backup showed as
  mojibake in the config/compare views -- the fetched config bytes are now
  decoded from UTF-8 before output, so they display correctly.
+ Devices page and the device-table editor's Devices tab: the pager gained
  First and Last buttons (in addition to Prev/Next) to jump straight to the
  first or last page of a long list.
+ Devices page: added a Hostname column (between Device-ID and Comment) with
  its own filter box (cookie-persisted like the others). Sorting the Hostname
  column puts IPv4 addresses first in numeric IP order, then hostnames/FQDNs
  alphabetically (IPv6 sorts with the names).
+ Device-table editor: the "+ Add model defaults" button (Defaults tab) now
  adds the new entry at the top of the list (like "+ Add device"), so it's
  visible without scrolling.
+ New device model "aruba-cx-ssh" (Aruba CX / AOS-CX over SSH). user/pass
  mandatory (no enable -- AOS-CX has none); show_cmd, debug and banner_timeout
  optional. Includes a dedicated AOS-CX syntax highlighter (! comments, a
  "!"-delimited banner, "ciphertext" credential blobs left plain, 1/1/1 and
  lag/vlan interfaces, IPv4/CIDR, single-quoted descriptions; no, user,
  loop-protect and ntp are coloured as command keywords like interface).
+ New device model "planet-ssh" (PLANET managed switches, Cisco-IOS-like CLI
  over SSH). user/pass mandatory; enable, show_cmd, debug, banner_timeout and
  type_delay (ms between typed characters) optional. Reuses the Cisco IOS
  syntax highlighter.
+ New device model "procurve-web-1700" (HP ProCurve 1700 series; web-managed,
  no CLI). Same binary/uuencoded web-UI handling as tplink-web-sg105e --
  Compare actions hidden, config view offers a Download of the transparently
  uudecoded binary ("<Device-ID>-switch.cfg"). The switch has only a manager
  password, so its "user" option is optional (ignored).
+ New device model "tplink-web-sg105e" (TP-Link Easy Smart switches, e.g.
  TL-SG105E). These have no CLI; fetchconfig scrapes the web GUI over HTTP.
  Options: user/pass/repository/keep/timeout mandatory, plus fetch_timeout,
  changes_only, on_fetch_run, on_fetch_cat, timezone, filename_append_suffix
  and debug=on|off optional.
+ Their backups are opaque binary files stored uuencoded, so for this model
  the web UI: (a) hides the Compare Selected / Side by Side Selected actions
  and the backup checkboxes (a line diff isn't meaningful), and (b) replaces
  the config text view with a page offering a Download button that
  transparently uudecodes the stored backup to the real binary, named
  "<Device-ID>-config.cfg" (what the switch's Config Restore page expects).

1.9
===
+ New device model "zyxel" in the editor catalog (user/pass/repository/keep/
  timeout mandatory; enable, show_cmd and debug=on|off optional), with a
  dedicated IOS-like syntax highlighter (handles the spaced
  "GigabitEthernet 1/1" unit and leaves the "password encrypted" blob
  un-tokenised).

1.8
===
+ Bulk Edit (Setup page, admin only): a plain-text editor for the device
  lines. Loads the complete device list (passwords in plain text); saving
  replaces the entire DEVICES SECTION, with all-or-nothing validation, the
  mtime guard, and a timestamped backup -- the default:/email: sections are
  left untouched.
+ Tools: "Devices without backups" and "Check devices for empty backups"
  scans -- check every Device-ID (via fetchconfig.pl -l and -z respectively)
  and list the flagged devices, with a live progress bar and a Start/Cancel
  button (client-driven, one read-only JSON request per device; admin only).
  Devices that can't be checked are listed by name.
* Fixed: "Check devices for empty backups" missed devices that actually had
  empty backups and reported them as errors -- the -z result was misparsed
  (a single-device -z line is "<num>\t<size>\t<path>", no dev-id) and -z's
  exit status 1 ("empties found") was treated as a failure.
+ Restore device table (Tools page, admin only): lists the timestamped
  backups in $BACKUP_DEVICE_TABLE with View / Restore / Delete. Restore
  backs up the current table first (so it's undoable); Restore and Delete
  each confirm. Backup names are strictly validated (no path traversal).
  A "current" row (the live table) and per-row checkboxes allow comparing
  any two configurations, via "Compare Selected" (unified diff, showing only
  changed lines) or "Side by Side Selected" (two-column). The newer config
  is always green, the older red (current = newest, else later timestamp),
  regardless of tick order; whitespace outside quotes is ignored
  (quote-aware). View and Compare mask passwords by default, with a "Mask
  passwords" checkbox to reveal them (the whole section is admin-only).
* Fixed: the unified "Compare Selected" diff legend said
  "added/removed/changed" but that view only colours old (red) vs new
  (green) -- the legend now reads "red = old, green = new". The Side by Side
  legend (added/removed/changed) is unchanged.
+ Device-table editor: the Devices tab gained Device-ID and comment filter
  boxes (substring, AND-combined, matched against live field values),
  remembered in a cookie; pagination now works over the filtered set.
+ Device-table editor (Setup -> Edit device table): a structured, three-tab
  editor (Devices / Defaults / Email) driven by a per-model option catalog,
  replacing hand-editing of the raw table. Masked-secret fields with a
  keep-on-save placeholder, per-model "+ add option" menus, host validation
  (IPv4/IPv6/FQDN), duplicate-Device-ID checks, a "Preview raw table" step
  (Save or Back from the preview), a modification-time concurrency guard,
  and a timestamped backup written to $BACKUP_DEVICE_TABLE before each save.
  New devices are added at the top; the Devices tab paginates at 100 per
  page. Option parsing/serialisation matches fetchconfig V9.38 exactly
  (Text::ParseWords, keep=1) including comment= and quoted values.
+ "admin_function" per-user right: a user with it is a full admin,
  equivalent to the built-in "admin" account (user management, Tools,
  Setup/editor). Managed from the User page's new "Admin functions" column
  and an Add-user checkbox; the built-in "admin" stays undeletable and
  always-on. New "admin_function" column in the users table (see README for
  the manual upgrade SQL).
+ Tools page (admins only): "Check for orphaned backups" (fetchconfig -o,
  read-only) and "Delete orphaned backups" (fetchconfig -o -D, confirmed
  first). Output shown like a Backup Now run.
+ Config syntax highlighting on the backup content view for cisco-ios and
  cisco-ios-ssh, via an extensible per-model highlighter registry.
  Certificate/key hex dumps and banner blocks are left un-highlighted.
  Highlighting is cosmetic only -- same escaping/secret-masking as the plain
  view; the Copy button still copies plain text.
+ New device model "hirschmann" in the editor catalog (user/pass/repository/
  keep/timeout mandatory; enable and show_cmd optional).
+ Device view: model filter is now a dropdown of the models in use; filter
  choices persist in a cookie across navigation; statistics panel moved to
  the upper-right and gained a live "devices filtered" count.
+ Floating "Top"/"Bottom" navigation buttons (bottom-right, clear of the
  Log out link) on the long views: device list, Setup, device-table editor,
  backup content view, and side-by-side diff.
+ Editor action buttons (Preview / Save without preview / Cancel) moved to
  the top of the editor.
+ User page: the "Change your password", "Users", and "Add user" areas are
  now visually separated boxes.
+ Device-table editor writes the table in a fixed section layout
  (DEFAULT OPTIONS SECTION / DEVICES SECTION), preserving option order and
  keeping unknown directives under a "# Unknown" heading, with a blank line
  between default groups of different models.
+ Editor backups now go to a configurable directory, BACKUP_DEVICE_TABLE
  (default /usr/local/fetchconfig/backup); the table is overwritten in
  place so only the file and the backup directory need to be web-writable.
* Fixed: the read-only "Compare" form is now submitted with the correct
  urlencoded enctype (was emitting multipart/form-data with method=GET,
  which Firefox warned about).

1.7
===
+ Intermediate development version introducing the first structured
  device-table editor; superseded by the consolidated 1.8 above.

1.6
===
+ User accounts moved from the htpasswd file to a PostgreSQL database
  (DBI/DBD::Pg). Standalone setup script fetchconfig-web-dbsetup.pl creates
  the database/table, can import existing htpasswd accounts, and can write
  /etc/fetchconfig.cfg. New-password hashing is pure-Perl Apache MD5
  ($apr1$), so the htpasswd binary and its sudo rule are no longer needed.
+ Per-user "edit_device_table" right (grantable from the User page) that
  also grants read access to the Setup page.
+ Default-password login warning until an account changes away from the
  default password.
+ All configuration moved to an external file, /etc/fetchconfig.cfg
  (key = value); only the config-file path, app title, and cookie name stay
  hard-coded.

1.5 and earlier
===============
+ Login, device list with live filter and sortable Model/Device-ID columns,
  per-device backup list with Date/Time columns, backup content view with
  copy-to-clipboard, unified "Compare" and "Side by Side" diff views,
  "Backup Now" and "Check Empty Backups", the read-only Setup page, in-UI
  user management, and the Help page. CSRF protection and server-side
  session store throughout.
